Real-World Example: E-Commerce Schema

Designing a robust and scalable database schema is crucial for any e-commerce platform. The schema must efficiently handle products, users, orders, payments, inventory, and more, while supporting complex queries and business logic.

📋 Overview
This example outlines a relational database schema for a typical e-commerce platform. It covers core entities, relationships, and key attributes to support common e-commerce functionalities like product catalog, user management, order processing, and payment tracking.

E-Commerce Schema Design
1. Core Entities and Their Purpose
Entity	Description
Users	Stores customer and admin user information
Products	Catalog of items available for sale
Categories	Product categorization for easier browsing
Orders	Customer orders with status tracking
OrderItems	Items within an order (many-to-one with Orders)
Payments	Payment transactions related to orders
Addresses	Shipping and billing addresses for users
Inventory	Tracks stock levels for each product
Reviews	Customer reviews and ratings for products
Coupons	Discount codes and promotions
2. Entity Relationship Diagram (ERD) - Simplified
