Real-World Considerations
UUIDs are used as primary keys for scalability and security.
price_at_order in OrderItems preserves historical pricing.
Inventory is separated to allow real-time stock updates without locking product data.
Addresses linked to users allow multiple shipping/billing addresses.
Coupons table supports promotions with usage limits.
Reviews enable social proof and product feedback.
Orders and Payments are decoupled to support multiple payment attempts or refunds.
Indexes should be added on foreign keys and frequently queried columns (e.g., email, order_status).
Example SQL DDL Snippet for Users and Products
Copy
sql
CREATE TABLE Users (
    user_id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
    email VARCHAR(255) UNIQUE NOT NULL,
    password_hash VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,
    full_name VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,
    phone VARCHAR(20),
    created_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
    is_admin BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE
);

CREATE TABLE Categories (
    category_id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
    name VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,
    parent_id UUID,
    FOREIGN KEY (parent_id) REFERENCES Categories(category_id)
);

CREATE TABLE Products (
    product_id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),
    name VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,
    description TEXT,
    price DECIMAL(10,2) NOT NULL,
    category_id UUID,
    created_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
    is_active BOOLEAN DEFAULT TRUE,
    FOREIGN KEY (category_id) REFERENCES Categories(category_id)
);
Summary
This schema provides a solid foundation for an e-commerce platform supporting:

User and admin management
Product catalog with categories and inventory
Order lifecycle with detailed items and payment tracking
Customer reviews and promotional coupons
Multiple addresses per user for shipping flexibility

